meta-rzg3l-seamless-logo-sequence
=================================

Seamless boot logo sequence for the RZ/G3L SMARC EVK. U-Boot plays a short
sequence of frames from a few hundred milliseconds after reset and stays on the
last one.
The kernel inherits the running display, so the last frame stays on screen
until the first program in Linux draws. There is no black screen and no HDMI
re-sync in between.

The layer contains no Linux application. To continue the sequence in Linux,
use rzg3l-seamless-logo-sequence-app or your own DRM/KMS application. The layer
and the application do not depend on each other.

Tested with Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0, u-boot 2024.07,
linux-renesas 6.12.46-cip8, MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l", DISTRO = "rz-vlp",
core-image-weston.


I. Boot sequence
================

  U-Boot     Brings up CPG, DSI D-PHY and link, ADV7535, DU and VSPD, loads
             frame 00 from /boot and starts the display on it.
             *** frame 00 visible ***
             The remaining frames are loaded behind it and played by the VSPD
             without CPU involvement. Each frame is held for logo_rate_ms;
             the last frame is held until Linux takes over.
  booti      U-Boot tells the kernel, through the device tree, that the
             display is running and which mode it uses (section V).
  Kernel     The frame buffers stay reserved, the display clocks stay on, and
             the display drivers inherit the running pipeline.
             *** last frame still visible ***
  Userspace  The first DRM commit (compositor or application) replaces the
             frame without a mode change.

The sequence plays in parallel with the boot. The only added time is loading
the frames (about 70 ms per frame at 1080p, after frame 00 is on screen).


II. Compatibility
=================

  meta-rzg3l-seamless-logo-still
      Cannot be used together with this layer. The build stops with an error
      if both are present.

  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot
      Independent. The combination is expected to work but is not tested.

  Applications
      The layer installs and starts nothing in Linux and does not modify
      weston-init. Any DRM/KMS client can take the display over. See
      section VII.


III. Build and flash
====================

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-seamless-logo-sequence
  $ bitbake core-image-weston

The frames are installed into /boot of the rootfs (partition 2 of the WIC
image). U-Boot is part of bl2/fip. How it reaches the board depends on the
boot mode (RZ/G3L Linux Start-up Guide):

  QSPI boot (default)
      Write bl2_bp_spi-smarc-rzg3l.srec and fip-smarc-rzg3l.srec with Flash
      Writer (Start-up Guide 4.3-4.5). Writing the WIC does NOT update U-Boot.
  eSD boot
      The WIC image contains bl2 and fip, so writing the WIC to the microSD
      card also updates U-Boot (Start-up Guide 8.3).
  eMMC boot
      Start-up Guide 8.2.

Write bl2/fip again after any change on the U-Boot side, including the
resolution or the frame time.

With a new WIC and an old or stock fip, the board boots normally: U-Boot
shows nothing and the kernel initialises the display itself.


IV. Configuration
=================

All settings go in conf/local.conf.

Frames
------

  RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_FRAMES = "l1.bmp l2.bmp l3.bmp l4.bmp"      (default)

The frames U-Boot plays, in this order. Each entry is a file name in this
layer's recipes-graphics/rzg3l-seamless-logo-sequence-frames/files (or a
bbappend's), or an absolute path on the build host:

  RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_FRAMES = "/home/user/logo/a.bmp /home/user/logo/b.bmp"

  - The number of frames is the number of entries.
  - Uncompressed 24 or 32 bpp BMP, exactly RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_RESOLUTION in
    size. The build checks this.
  - Maximum 14 frames at 1920x1080, 31 frames at 1280x720.

  RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_PACKAGE = ""

builds an image without frames. U-Boot then shows a black frame and the
hand-over still takes place.

Frame time
----------

  RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_RATE_MS = "1000"                              (default)

Time each frame is held, in milliseconds, compiled into U-Boot. Rounded down
to whole output frames (16.7 ms at 60 Hz).

Resolution
----------

  RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_RESOLUTION = "1920x1080"                      (default)
  RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_RESOLUTION = "1280x720"

The mode U-Boot drives. The kernel needs no change. Provide frames of the
same size.

U-Boot does not read EDID; each resolution is a compiled-in timing table. To
add one, add a U-Boot patch after 0010 that:

  1. Copies drivers/video/rzg2l/define_1080p.h to a new header and sets the
     PLL7, D-PHY, DSI link, DU and ADV7535 timings and LCD_CEA_VIC for the
     mode. The VIC must be a CEA-861 mode with the same timing.
  2. Adds the header to the table in drivers/video/rzg2l/rzg2l_video.c:

       #elif (RZG2L_LOGO_WIDTH == 1024) && (RZG2L_LOGO_HEIGHT == 768)
       #include "define_1024x768.h"

The ADV7535 supports up to 148.5 MHz pixel clock (1920x1080@60). The build
reports an error if RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_RESOLUTION has no table.

U-Boot environment
------------------

For tests on the board without a rebuild (setenv, then saveenv):

  logo_rate_ms     frame time in ms; overrides RZG3L_LOGO_SEQUENCE_RATE_MS
  logo_frames      play at most this many frames
  logo_mmc_dev     0 (eMMC) or 1 (microSD); detected automatically if unset
  logo_handover    "no" leaves the display to the kernel (stock behaviour)

The variables are read at power-on, before bootcmd runs.


V. Hand-over declaration
========================

The kernel drivers inherit the display only if their device-tree nodes carry
"renesas,seamless-boot-logo". U-Boot adds these properties at booti, and only
after it has started the display:

  /soc/vsp@10870000            renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  /soc/display@10890000        renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  /soc/dsi@10850000            renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  /soc/i2c@10090800/hdmi@3d    renesas,seamless-boot-logo
                               renesas,seamless-boot-logo-vic = <VIC>
                               interrupts-extended deleted

The same kernel therefore boots correctly with any bootloader. If the
declaration is not made, U-Boot prints a "fast-logo:" line with the reason.


VI. Verification
================

U-Boot console:

  fast-logo: 1000 ms per frame
  fast-logo: intro from mmc 1:2
  fast-logo: intro is 4 frame(s)
  fast-logo: intro armed, <n> headers, parks on frame 3
  ...
  fast-logo: hand-over declared to the kernel, CEA VIC 16
  Starting kernel ...

Linux:

  # ls /proc/device-tree/soc/i2c@10090800/hdmi@3d/ | grep seamless
  renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  renesas,seamless-boot-logo-vic
  # ls /boot/fastlogo_*
  # cat /sys/class/drm/card0-HDMI-A-1/modes
  1920x1080

Build:

  $ strings tmp/deploy/images/smarc-rzg3l/fip.bin | grep fastlogo_
  boot/fastlogo_%ux%u_%02u.bin.gz


VII. Notes for applications
===========================

  - The HDMI connector reports one mode, the one U-Boot drives, and is always
    connected. Use that mode; other modes are rejected.
  - The first DRM commit takes the display over. Nothing may commit before
    the program that is meant to own the display.
  - Only one program can be DRM master. If the compositor also starts, order
    the application before it (Before=weston.service) and release DRM master
    before the compositor needs it.
  - There is no /dev/fb0. Draw through DRM/KMS.


VIII. Known limitations
=======================

  - The HDMI connector is pinned to the U-Boot mode for the lifetime of the
    system: always connected, no EDID, no hot-plug events. A display that
    does not support the mode shows no picture.
  - seamless-logo-sequence.cfg disables the framebuffer console, the kernel
    logo, DRM fbdev emulation and CONFIG_VT_CONSOLE, because each would draw
    over the logo. As a result there is no /dev/fb0 and no kernel messages on
    HDMI. The serial console is not affected. The build fails if any of these
    options is enabled in the final kernel configuration.
  - The "splash" image feature is removed (conf/layer.conf).
  - 0x58000000..0x5fffffff (128 MiB) stays reserved, and the MIPI DSI and
    LCDC clocks stay on, for the lifetime of the system.
  - The BSP U-Boot environment has loadaddr=0x58000000, which is frame 00. A
    manual "ext4load mmc 0:2 - <file>" or "tftp <file>" at the U-Boot prompt
    overwrites the picture. Give an explicit address.
  - The frames are loaded from partition 2 (ext4) of the MMC device the board
    boots from. Other boot media show a black frame.
  - The hand-over is declared by the booti/bootm device-tree fix-up. Other
    boot paths (for example bootefi, not tested) do not declare it; the
    kernel then initialises the display itself.
